5 #edb/ref 17592186476240 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476868 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186476502 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476868 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186476538 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476868 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186476645 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476868 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186476754 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476868 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476868 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476868 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186476538 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476868 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "fcdfbcb7-b6c6-5e73-9143-d0c9ce607525" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476875 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186476862 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476875 #edb/ref 1069 "q-142857" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476875 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476875 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}2y, & 0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe marginal CDF ${F}_{X}(x)$ of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\n{F}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x<0 \\\\ g(x), & 0\\le x\\le 1 \\\\ 1 & x>1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhat is the function $g(x)?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476875 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "076df80a-4584-5238-8a7e-ff37062cbf3b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476875 #edb/ref 1074 "If $X$ and $Y$ are two continuous random variables with joint probability density function $f(x,y),$ then the marginal density functions for $X$ and $Y$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x)={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y,\\,{f}_{Y}(y)={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the marginal density function of $X$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x) & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}2y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2{[\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =[{y}^{2}]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =({1}^{2}-0) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the marginal CDF of $X$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}_{X}(x) & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}{f}_{X}(u)\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{x}1\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =[u]_{0}^{x} \\\\  & =x-0 \\\\  & =x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the full expression for ${F}_{X}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{F}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x<0 \\\\ x, & 0\\le x\\le 1 \\\\ 1 & x>1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $g(x)=x.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476884 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186476809 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476884 #edb/ref 1069 "q-142923" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476884 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476884 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{(x+y)^{3}}, & x\\ge 1,\\,y\\ge 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe marginal CDF ${F}_{X}(x)$ of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\n{F}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x<1 \\\\ g(x), & x\\ge 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhat is the function $g(x)?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476884 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "31433c7d-56df-5a56-80c3-21c7e39be82f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476884 #edb/ref 1074 "If $X$ and $Y$ are two continuous random variables with joint probability density function $f(x,y),$ then the marginal density functions for $X$ and $Y$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x)={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y,\\,{f}_{Y}(y)={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the marginal density function of $X$ for $x\\ge 1$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x) & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{(x+y)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={lim}_{b\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{b}\\frac{4}{(x+y)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =4{lim}_{b\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{b}(x+y)^{-3}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =4{lim}_{b\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{1}{2}(x+y)^{-2}]}_{y=1}^{y=b} \\\\  & =-2{lim}_{b\\to \\infty }{[\\frac{1}{(x+y)^{2}}]}_{y=1}^{y=b} \\\\  & =-2{lim}_{b\\to \\infty }[\\frac{1}{(x+b)^{2}}-\\frac{1}{(x+1)^{2}}] \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{(x+1)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the marginal CDF of $X$ for $x\\ge 1$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}_{X}(x) & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}{f}_{X}(u)\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{x}\\frac{2}{(u+1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{1}^{x}\\frac{1}{(u+1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =2{[-\\frac{1}{u+1}]}_{1}^{x} \\\\  & =2[\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{x+1}] \\\\  & =1-\\frac{2}{x+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{x-1}{x+1},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the full expression for ${F}_{X}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{F}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x<1 \\\\ \\frac{x-1}{x+1}, & x\\ge 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $g(x)=\\frac{x-1}{x+1}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476885 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186476856 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476885 #edb/ref 1069 "q-142965" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476885 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476885 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-(\\frac{x}{2}+2y)}, & x\\ge 0,\\,y\\ge 0, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind $P(Y>2).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476885 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "335726b7-e5f3-5cb6-916f-382cd146373a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476885 #edb/ref 1074 "If $X$ and $Y$ are two continuous random variables with joint probability density function $f(x,y),$ then the marginal density functions for $X$ and $Y$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x)={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y,\\,{f}_{Y}(y)={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the marginal density function of $Y$ for $y\\ge 0$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-(\\frac{x}{2}+2y)}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[-2{e}^{-(\\frac{x}{2}+2y)}]}_{x=0}^{x=\\infty } \\\\  & =-2(0-{e}^{-(0+2y)}) \\\\  & =2{e}^{-2y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the full expression for ${f}_{Y}(y)$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{-2y}, & y\\ge 0, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the required probability is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y>2) & ={\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }{f}_{Y}(y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }2{e}^{-2y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[-{e}^{-2y}]}_{2}^{\\infty } \\\\  & =(0+{e}^{-4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{e}^{4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476889 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186476762 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476889 #edb/ref 1069 "q-142861" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476889 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476889 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x+y}, & x\\le 0,\\,y\\le 0, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe marginal CDF ${F}_{Y}(y)$ of $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\n{F}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}g(y), & y\\le 0 \\\\ 1 & y>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhat is the function $g(y)?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476889 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4107781e-c996-571c-b9f1-cc16eec90a01" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476889 #edb/ref 1074 "If $X$ and $Y$ are two continuous random variables with joint probability density function $f(x,y),$ then the marginal density functions for $X$ and $Y$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x)={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y,\\,{f}_{Y}(y)={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the marginal density function of $Y$ for $y\\le 0$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}{e}^{x+y}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{y}{lim}_{b\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{b}^{0}{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{y}{lim}_{b\\to -\\infty }{[{e}^{x}]}_{b}^{0} \\\\  & ={e}^{y}{lim}_{b\\to -\\infty }(1-{e}^{b}) \\\\  & ={e}^{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the marginal CDF of $Y$ for $y\\le 0$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}_{Y}(y) & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{y}{f}_{Y}(u)\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{y}{e}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{b\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{b}^{y}{e}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{b\\to -\\infty }{[{e}^{u}]}_{b}^{y} \\\\  & ={lim}_{b\\to -\\infty }({e}^{y}-{e}^{b}) \\\\  & ={e}^{y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the full expression for ${F}_{Y}(y)$ is\n\n$$\n{F}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{y}, & y\\le 0 \\\\ 1 & y>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $g(y)={e}^{y}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476909 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186476826 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476909 #edb/ref 1069 "q-142967" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476909 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476909 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}-1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+y), & 0\\le x\\le \\frac{\\pi }{4},\\,0\\le y\\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind $P(-\\frac{\\pi }{6}<Y\\le \\frac{\\pi }{12}).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476909 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6d033f40-c93e-54e7-8136-01042b0b61b2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476909 #edb/ref 1074 "If $X$ and $Y$ are two continuous random variables with joint probability density function $f(x,y),$ then the marginal density functions for $X$ and $Y$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x)={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y,\\,{f}_{Y}(y)={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the marginal density function of $Y$ for $0\\le y\\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}-1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+y)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}-1}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)]_{x=0}^{x=\\pi /4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}-1}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}+y)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0+y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}-1}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}+y)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡y).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the full expression for ${f}_{Y}(y)$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}-1}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}+y)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡y), & 0\\le y\\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the required probability is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-\\frac{\\pi }{6}<Y\\le \\frac{\\pi }{12}) & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{\\pi /12}{f}_{Y}(y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /12}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}-1}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}+y)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}-1}{[-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}+y)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡y]}_{0}^{\\pi /12} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}-1}[(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\frac{\\pi }{12})+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{12})-(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}+0)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡0)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}-1}(-\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1+\\sqrt{3}}{2\\sqrt{2}}+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}-1) \\\\  & ≈0.4177.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476911 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186476868 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476911 #edb/ref 1069 "q-145015" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476911 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476911 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x{y}^{2}}{4}, & \\,1\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 2 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the expression for the marginal PDF ${f}_{Y}(y).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476911 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7040e2bd-4a32-5fec-9df4-e31bdd95ffae" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476911 #edb/ref 1074 "If $X$ and $Y$ are two continuous random variables with joint probability density function $f(x,y),$ then the marginal density functions for $X$ and $Y$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x)={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y,\\,{f}_{Y}(y)={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the marginal density function of $Y$ for $0\\le y\\le 2$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{x{y}^{2}}{4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}{\\int }_{1}^{2}x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}{[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}]}_{x=1}^{x=2} \\\\  & =\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}[2-\\frac{1}{2}] \\\\  & =\\frac{3{y}^{2}}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the full expression for ${f}_{Y}(y)$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3{y}^{2}}{8}, & \\,0\\le y\\le 2 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476913 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186476813 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476913 #edb/ref 1069 "q-142872" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476913 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476913 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}-1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+y), & 0\\le x\\le \\frac{\\pi }{4},\\,0\\le y\\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the expression for the marginal PDF ${f}_{Y}(y).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476913 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7462dbdf-074b-5e9c-88bb-4a4709315e55" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476913 #edb/ref 1074 "If $X$ and $Y$ are two continuous random variables with joint probability density function $f(x,y),$ then the marginal density functions for $X$ and $Y$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x)={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y,\\,{f}_{Y}(y)={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the marginal density function of $Y$ for $0\\le y\\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}-1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+y)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}-1}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)]_{x=0}^{x=\\pi /4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}-1}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}+y)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0+y)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}-1}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}+y)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡y).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the full expression for ${f}_{Y}(y)$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}-1}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}+y)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡y), & 0\\le y\\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476914 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186476819 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476914 #edb/ref 1069 "q-142916" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476914 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476914 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y\\sqrt{x}}{9}, & 0\\le x\\le 9,\\,0\\le y\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe marginal CDF ${F}_{Y}(y)$ of $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\n{F}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & y<0 \\\\ g(y), & 0\\le y\\le 1 \\\\ 1 & y>1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhat is the function $g(y)?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476914 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "74d58075-0369-56a0-b633-ba74268879be" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476914 #edb/ref 1074 "If $X$ and $Y$ are two continuous random variables with joint probability density function $f(x,y),$ then the marginal density functions for $X$ and $Y$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y)={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y,\\,{f}_{Y}(y)={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the marginal density function of $Y$ for $0\\le y\\le 1$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={\\int }_{0}^{9}\\frac{y\\sqrt{x}}{9}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{y}{9}{\\int }_{0}^{9}\\sqrt{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{y}{9}{[\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{3/2}]}_{x=0}^{x=9} \\\\  & =\\frac{2y}{27}{[{x}^{3/2}]}_{x=0}^{x=9} \\\\  & =\\frac{2y}{27}({9}^{3/2}-{0}^{3/2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2y}{27}(27-0) \\\\  & =2y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the marginal CDF of $Y$ for $0\\le y\\le 1$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}_{Y}(y) & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{y}{f}_{Y}(u)\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{y}2u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={[{u}^{2}]}_{0}^{y} \\\\  & ={y}^{2}-0 \\\\  & ={y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the full expression for ${F}_{Y}(y)$ is\n\n$$\n{F}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & y<0 \\\\ {y}^{2}, & 0\\le y\\le 1 \\\\ 1 & y>1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $g(y)={y}^{2}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476931 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186476858 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476931 #edb/ref 1069 "q-142888" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476931 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476931 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}x+y, & 0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the expression for the marginal PDF ${f}_{Y}(y).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476931 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a1209ac5-9324-5c4f-8a14-c5d815ce9e79" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476931 #edb/ref 1074 "If $X$ and $Y$ are two continuous random variables with joint probability density function $f(x,y),$ then the marginal density functions for $X$ and $Y$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x)={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y,\\,{f}_{Y}(y)={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the marginal density function of $Y$ for $0\\le y\\le 1$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(x+y)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+xy]}_{x=0}^{x=1} \\\\  & =[(\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {1}^{2}+1\\cdot y)-(\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {0}^{2}+0\\cdot y)] \\\\  & =y+\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the full expression for ${f}_{Y}(y)$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}y+\\frac{1}{2}, & 0\\le y\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476940 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186476837 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476940 #edb/ref 1069 "q-142754" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476940 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476940 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+\\frac{8}{9}xy, & 0\\le x\\le 1,\\,1\\le y\\le 2, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the expression for the marginal PDF ${f}_{X}(x).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476940 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "acc3ecee-2ca3-52a4-a564-fe35d9f4b095" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476940 #edb/ref 1074 "If $X$ and $Y$ are two continuous random variables with joint probability density function $f(x,y),$ then the marginal density functions for $X$ and $Y$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x)={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y,\\,{f}_{Y}(y)={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the marginal density function of $X$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x) & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}({x}^{2}+\\frac{8}{9}xy)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[{x}^{2}y+\\frac{4}{9}x{y}^{2}]}_{y=1}^{y=2} \\\\  & =({x}^{2}(2)+\\frac{4}{9}x\\cdot {2}^{2})-({x}^{2}(1)+\\frac{4}{9}x\\cdot {1}^{2}) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+\\frac{4}{3}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the full expression for ${f}_{X}(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+\\frac{4}{3}x, & 0\\le x\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476942 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186476802 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476942 #edb/ref 1069 "q-142911" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476942 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476942 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-(5x+\\frac{y}{5})}, & x\\ge 0,\\,y\\ge 0, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind $P(X\\le 5).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476942 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "aecec449-e3ae-5803-8a70-f99f6be91bad" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476942 #edb/ref 1074 "If $X$ and $Y$ are two continuous random variables with joint probability density function $f(x,y),$ then the marginal density functions for $X$ and $Y$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x)={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y,\\,{f}_{Y}(y)={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the marginal density function of $X$ for $x\\ge 0$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-(5x+\\frac{y}{5})}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[-5{e}^{-(5x+\\frac{y}{5})}]}_{y=0}^{y=\\infty } \\\\  & =-5(0-{e}^{-(5x+0)}) \\\\  & =5{e}^{-5x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the full expression for ${f}_{X}(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}5{e}^{-5x}, & x\\ge 0, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the required probability is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 5) & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{5}{f}_{X}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{5}5{e}^{-5x}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[-{e}^{-5x}]}_{0}^{5} \\\\  & =-{e}^{-25}+{e}^{0} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{1}{{e}^{25}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476947 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186476817 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476947 #edb/ref 1069 "q-142710" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476947 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476947 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with a joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\frac{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}{3},\\,0\\le 3y\\le x\\le 3.\n$$\n\nFind the expression for the marginal PDF ${f}_{Y}(y).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476947 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b503f4b2-2e6a-599e-9f9e-bfcbf92fe455" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476947 #edb/ref 1074 "If $X$ and $Y$ are two continuous random variables with joint probability density function $f(x,y),$ then the marginal density functions for $X$ and $Y$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x)={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y,\\,{f}_{Y}(y)={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the region $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le 3y\\le x\\le 3\\}.$\n\n![](images/54/54c803be9ecc7873f2624fc9d083997039cb651c11cc38eff6fa561ec064b7a5.png)\n\nIn our case, the marginal density function of $Y$ for $0\\le y\\le 1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={\\int }_{3y}^{3}\\frac{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{3y}^{3}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{[\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}+x{y}^{2}]}_{x=3y}^{x=3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {3}^{3}+3\\cdot {y}^{2}-\\frac{1}{3}(3y)^{3}-3y\\cdot {y}^{2}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[9+3{y}^{2}-12{y}^{3}] \\\\  & =3+{y}^{2}-4{y}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the full expression for ${f}_{Y}(y)$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}3+{y}^{2}-4{y}^{3}, & 0\\le y\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476948 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186476823 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476948 #edb/ref 1069 "q-142924" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476948 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476948 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}, & x\\ge 1,\\,y\\ge 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe marginal CDF ${F}_{X}(x)$ of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\n{F}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x<1 \\\\ g(x), & x\\ge 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhat is the function $g(x)?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476948 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b66f8317-7401-5c42-a057-174f43015e08" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476948 #edb/ref 1074 "If $X$ and $Y$ are two continuous random variables with joint probability density function $f(x,y),$ then the marginal density functions for $X$ and $Y$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x)={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y,\\,{f}_{Y}(y)={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the marginal density function of $X$ for $x\\ge 1$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x) & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}{lim}_{b\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{b}\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}{lim}_{b\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{1}{y}]}_{y=1}^{y=b} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}{lim}_{b\\to \\infty }[1-\\frac{1}{b}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the marginal CDF of $X$ for $x\\ge 1$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}_{X}(x) & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}{f}_{X}(u)\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{x}\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{1}{u}]}_{1}^{x} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{1}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{x-1}{x},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the full expression for ${F}_{X}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{F}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x<1 \\\\ \\frac{x-1}{x}, & x\\ge 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $g(x)=\\frac{x-1}{x}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476954 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186476781 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476954 #edb/ref 1069 "q-142951" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476954 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476954 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{21}(xy)^{2}, & 0\\le x\\le 3,\\,1\\le y\\le 2, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind $P(X>1).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476954 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "bdb3668b-11de-51d3-b228-d2e5f99dedeb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476954 #edb/ref 1074 "If $X$ and $Y$ are two continuous random variables with joint probability density function $f(x,y),$ then the marginal density functions for $X$ and $Y$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x)={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y,\\,{f}_{Y}(y)={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the marginal density function of $X$ for $0\\le x\\le 3$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x) & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{21}(xy)^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{21}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{21}{[\\frac{1}{3}{y}^{3}]}_{y=1}^{y=2} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{63}({2}^{3}-{1}^{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the full expression for ${f}_{X}(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{9}{x}^{2}, & 0\\le x\\le 3, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the required probability is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>1) & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{f}_{X}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}\\frac{1}{9}{x}^{2}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}{[\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}]}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{27}[{3}^{3}-{1}^{3}] \\\\  & =\\frac{26}{27}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
